{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiF1PF6QnTmVVwVCzr1R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote `scorer_out.npz` and `NOTES.md` (198 words). It is a partial fix: about **70% of new bad-rate complaints** now go to fees_fx, and **about 1% of other messages** change route.\n\n**→ What changed.** I rewired one hidden unit in the main scorer, unit 234. It was dead: its input was at most −4.1 on all 3,345 vectors I tested. Now it adds +4 to the fees_fx score when that option's vector looks like a complaint about a bad exchange rate. Nothing else changed. I did not add an extra module. A check of the file confirms that only unit 234's weights differ, and that scores move by less than 0.0005 when the unit does not fire.\n\n**→ How I found the direction.** I took the average fees_fx-option vector of ~190 complaints I wrote, minus the average of every other group. Those groups are the other options of the same complaints, ~480 messages that cover all common banking topics, and currency questions as a separate group. The few highest-variance directions hide the signal, so I removed them. No labels were used for that step. The threshold sits at the 99.5th percentile of non-target vectors. No training or fitting was used.\n\n**→ Results** (`check.py` on sets I did not use to build the direction):\n\n| Check | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| New complaints routed to fees_fx (30) | 27% | 70% |\n| Evidence accuracy (32) | 50% | 94% |\n| Broad messages unchanged (300) | — | 99.3% |\n| Hard look-alikes unchanged (90) | — | 95.6% |\n\n**→ Known gaps.** About 30% of complaints still go to disputes, mostly ones worded with \"conversion\" instead of \"exchange rate\". Most wrong flips are currency questions such as \"swap pounds to euros in app\", but one is a real disputes message: \"I paid for something twice by mistake\". I rejected a stronger boost of 6: it raised recall but also flipped a real disputes example from the evidence.\n\n**→ Why not higher.** The marker vectors do not show which option they belong to in any linear way, and the complaint signal lives in low-variance directions. A two-layer \"AND-NOT\" gate gained only about 1 point, so I kept the simpler exact version.\n\n**Mistakes and guards:**\n- **Mistake:** a `try/except ValueError` hid a shape bug and reported \"no gap\" for every setting. **Guard:** I removed the catch, so errors now surface.\n- **Mistake:** one setting gave a negative slope and the build still saved a file. **Guard:** `build.py` now refuses to save if the median complaint is not above the threshold. A test run confirmed it refuses.\n- **Mistake:** I chose an output name, `neg_test.npz`, that is the same file as `NEG_TEST.npz` on this disk because macOS names ignore case. The guard fired before any write, so no data was lost. **Guard:** output names must not match any data file name. This project has no lessons file, so I suggest adding `LESSONS.md` next to `TASK.md` for steps 2 and 3.\n\nThe build scripts and probe messages are in `<tmp> To rebuild, run `build.py 99.5 4 <out>`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":549,"cache_read_input_tokens":222880,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":549},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e817e524-3b77-4db6-a889-79562c904b10","uuid":"d364769c-aa3b-4c97-aac1-571198041df3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:58:09.732Z","request_id":"req_011CgiF1Ns2U1UiMAqn2LHKY"}
